</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PID-AcT cuts toxicity by up to 8<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving utility.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Toxicity Mitigation ‣ 5 Controlling the Steering Effect ‣ Activation Steering with a Feedback Controller" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it lowers scores by <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8.1<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> on Gemma2-2B and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7.3<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> on LLaMA3-8B, consistently outperforming Mean-AcT and Linear-AcT. It achieves the lowest toxicity under both classifier and LLM-judge evaluations, while maintaining utility: MMLU drops <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>1% and perplexity rises only modestly (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>6%). Unlike sequential methods that plateau, PID’s integral-derivative dynamics deliver stronger, more stable mitigation without harming performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Jailbreaking Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on ActAdd within the Angular Steering framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu &amp; Nguyen, 2025</a>)</cite> on the jailbreaking task, which seeks to override a model’s refusal behavior and elicit harmful outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu &amp; Nguyen, 2025</a>)</cite>, we replace DIM with our method and baselines RePE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> and ITI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Refusal directions are built from 80% of <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AdvBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> and 512 harmless <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alpaca</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al., 2023</a>)</cite> samples, with the remaining 20% for evaluation. General LM ability is tested on <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TinyBenchmarks</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maia Polo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We evaluate across Gemma2, LLaMA3, and Qwen2.5 models (3B–14B).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of Original, DIM, ITI, RePE, and PID across models on ASR and general benchmarks. Bold = best, underline = second-best within each model (ASR column). Refer to Tab. <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ C.2 Jailbreaking Large Language Models ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Results ‣ Activation Steering with a Feedback Controller" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for results on all tested models.</figcaption>
